## Problem 6b {#problem-6b}

*If it does, what was the most frequent reason for each departure airport? Output airport name,
the most frequent reason, and the number of cancelations for that reason.*

The query counts cancellations by (airport, reason), ranks the reasons within each airport and
keeps rank 1. 296 airports had at least one cancellation. At 5 of them two reasons tie
for first place, and both are listed, so the result has **301 rows**. The first 20 are shown below; the
CSV has all of them.

::: {#56ff1836 .cell execution_count=24}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown execution_count=24}
```sql
WITH airport_reason AS (
    SELECT p.Origin AS airport_code,
           p.CancellationCode,
           COUNT(*) AS num_cancellations
    FROM al_perf p
    WHERE p.Cancelled = 1
    GROUP BY p.Origin, p.CancellationCode
),
ranked AS (
    SELECT airport_code, CancellationCode, num_cancellations,
           RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY airport_code ORDER BY num_cancellations DESC) AS rnk
    FROM airport_reason
)
SELECT ap.Name AS airport_name,
       c.Reason AS most_frequent_reason,
       r.num_cancellations
FROM ranked r
JOIN L_AIRPORT ap ON ap.Code = r.airport_code
JOIN L_CANCELATION c ON c.Code = r.CancellationCode
WHERE r.rnk = 1
ORDER BY r.num_cancellations DESC, airport_name, most_frequent_reason;
```
